<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  How a generated workspace pins its package source (FS.GG.Templates#384, the same
  root cause as #380).

  A committed `packages.lock.json` pins a package's IDENTITY, VERSION and CONTENT
  HASH — but not where that content is fetched from. Without this file a product
  inherits whatever `~/.nuget/NuGet/NuGet.Config` the machine happens to carry.
  The FS-GG dev container, for one, maps the `*` pattern to BOTH nuget.org and a
  local folder feed, so a package present in that folder feed is served from there
  instead. Its bytes are a different archive, so its content hash differs, and a
  later locked restore that resolves the same version from nuget.org fails with:

      error NU1403: Package content hash validation failed for <id>.<version>.
                    The package is different than the last restore.

  `<clear />` is the load-bearing line: it drops every inherited source so this
  workspace resolves from exactly one place, on every machine, forever. Deleting it
  makes the lockfiles next to it unenforceable again.

  Note what this file does NOT cover: a consumer's own probe feed. The composition
  lane packs this library and restores it from a local folder feed through its own
  `NuGet.Config`, which is correct — that consumer is a separate workspace with a
  separate lock, and pinning happens there, not here.

  Restoring from a private or mirrored feed
  =========================================
  Add the feed below and keep `<clear />`, then REGENERATE the lockfiles from that
  feed so the committed hashes describe the bytes it actually serves:

      dotnet restore BindingsProduct.slnx /p:RestoreForceEvaluate=true

  Adding a feed WITHOUT regenerating is the failure this file exists to prevent.
-->
<configuration>
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>
</configuration>
